{{see|Equus}}
==Latin==

===Alternative spellings===
* [[equos]] ''(till the middle of the first century B.C.)''
* [[ecus]] ''(the regular development, later remodeled to ''equus'' on the analogy of the stem ''equ-'' as remained, e.g., in the genitive'' equi.)

===Etymology===
From {{proto|Indo-European|h₁eḱwos|horse|lang=la}}. In the segmentation, *eḱw-o-, the American Heritage Dictionary relates *ekw- to the adjective, '''*oku-''', ''swift.'' A horse is thus named after its swiftness in Proto-Indo-European. Cognates include Mycenaean Greek {{term|𐀂𐀦|tr=i-qo|sc=Linb|lang=gmy}}, Aeolic Greek [[ἴκκος]] (''hikkos''), Ancient Greek [[ἵππος]] (''hippos''), Old English [[eoh]], Old Irish [[ech]], Sanskrit {{term|अश्व|tr=aśva|sc=Deva|lang=sa}}.

